diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase investigation found no evidence of any OSPF instability, past or present. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) returned 0 flagged devices: no ACL rules, no ARP anomalies, no addressing issues, no duplicate MACs, and no carrier transitions on any active interface. All DOWN interfaces belong to links explicitly labeled "unused" in the topology, which is expected baseline behavior. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed that all 9 FRR routers are healthy — vtysh responds, both control sockets are present, every adjacency is Full, and each router holds exactly 18 OSPF routes; no qdisc shaping exists anywhere. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, flap injection processes, or resource pressure on any of the 28 devices. Targeted follow-up on the specifically suspected links (router7↔router8 via eth4/eth5, router8↔router17 via eth0, and the mswitch0↔router16 LACP bond) showed all interfaces UP with zero errors, zero carrier changes, and normal OSPF hello packet counts. FRR log files and syslog on all three devices are empty for OSPF neighbor-state-change events. There is no mechanism capable of producing an adjacency bounce (no flap process, no timer mismatch, no link instability, no TC-injected jitter), and no historical trace that a bounce ever occurred. The NOC report of a "flapping OSPF area" is unconfirmed — the network is operating correctly in its current state.
=== END ===